.\" Manual page for hddscan.
.\" Keep in sync with usage() in hddscan.c; the two are checked against each
.\" other by the test in AGENTS.md.
.TH HDDSCAN 8 "2026-08-24" "hddscan 1.0" "System Administration Utilities"
.SH NAME
hddscan \- per-sector latency and error scanner for rotating disks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hddscan
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ device ...]
.br
.B hddscan --list
.br
.B hddscan --check-deps
.br
.B hddscan --status
.RI [ run ]
.br
.B hddscan dm
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.RB ( dm\-badblocks
.I command
when installed as that link)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hddscan
finds sectors on a rotating disk that return data too slowly or not at all.
It is aimed at the failure that SMART misses entirely: a drive that still
reads every sector successfully, but takes tens of milliseconds of internal
recovery to do it. Those sectors are the ones about to become unreadable.
.PP
Run on a terminal with no device named, hddscan opens an interactive form to
pick drives and settings, then shows a live dashboard while it runs. When
every drive finishes, a summary holds the verdicts on screen until a key is
pressed:
.B n
returns to the form to start another test,
.B q
quits and leaves the last run's full reports in the terminal. On a drive the
scan found damage on, having covered the whole drive,
.B h
hides that damage behind a device-mapper device, as
.B \-\-hide\-bad
does, after a
.B y
to confirm;
.BR Up / Down
choose the drive.
.PP
The work itself does not belong to that terminal. Each scan or format is a run
owned by a supervisor process of its own, so quitting, closing the window or
losing the connection leaves it going; starting hddscan again shows what is in
progress before it offers to configure anything, and
.B \-\-status
answers the same question in a script. Naming a
device, or
.BR --all ,
or
.BR --list ,
is an explicit instruction and is carried out as given, so nothing in a script
changes.
.BR --no-tui
suppresses the form entirely.
.PP
Devices may be given as
.IR /dev/sdb ,
as
.IR sdb ,
or as a regular file to use as a disk image. Image files exercise every code
path except the ones that need real hardware, which is how most of the tool is
tested.
.SS The guarantee
A chunk is never faster than its slowest sector. hddscan reads in bulk chunks
and times each one; a chunk that meets its latency budget therefore proves
every sector inside it did too, and needs no further attention. A chunk that
misses is re-read one block at a time until the responsible sectors are
identified, and each of those is retried. A sector that never answers is
.BR bad ;
one that answers late, or only on a retry, is
.BR weak .
How many attempts it took, and how many failed outright, is kept per sector
in the report and in
.BR \-\-csv .
.PP
Two things would break that guarantee, and both are handled. The drive's own
read look-ahead can serve a marginal sector out of DRAM at full speed, so it is
switched off for the duration of the scan and restored afterwards. And the
kernel page cache would do the same, so all reads use
.BR O_DIRECT .
If look-ahead could not be turned off and the scan order is sequential, the
report says so rather than presenting a result the tool cannot stand behind.
.SS What the live columns mean
.B RATE
and
.B ETA
are measured over the last 30 seconds, like the latency columns beside them:
on a scan measured in days a lifetime average cannot move, and the question
being asked of a dashboard is what the drive is doing now. The throughput
floor behind
.B too slow
is deliberately not windowed that way \(em see
.BR \-\-min\-rate .
.PP
The latency beside or under each drive is measured on the chunk reads that
finished inside the window, in milliseconds, with
.B wmed
the median write. A drive drilling into a slow chunk for longer than the
window finishes no chunk inside it, so the row shows the single\-block reads of
the drill\-down instead, marked
.BR sector ;
a drive that finished no read at all says that. A figure over 50 ms is yellow
and one over 100 ms red. The marks are fixed, so a number is the same colour
on every drive, and they are a cue rather than a verdict: the budget that
decides what is slow follows the platter, and a large chunk from the inner
tracks of a healthy drive can take more than 50 ms.
.PP
.B BAD
counts sectors that failed every attempt;
.B WEAK
counts sectors that were over the latency budget, or came back only on a
retry. Both are counted in sectors of
.BR \-\-block
bytes, and once either is not zero it is coloured, red and yellow. The end of
scan report breaks the same sectors down by how many tries and how many errors
each one took.
.SS Thresholds
Budgets are calibrated per drive, using the same access pattern the scan will
use. Timing scattered reads and then scanning sequentially would produce a
budget inflated by the cost of a seek \(em an order of magnitude on real
hardware, and far too loose to catch a sector needing 30 ms of recovery.
.PP
The budget scales with
.BR --chunk .
It is a multiple of the measured median chunk, so a larger chunk is much faster
but raises the bar a sector must clear before anything looks at it. On a
7200 rpm drive, 128 KiB calibrates to roughly 26 ms and 4 MiB to roughly
90 ms; a sector taking 60 ms is found by the first and hidden by the second.
The scan states the resulting sensitivity before it starts.
.BR --sector-slow-ms
pins the sector budget independently, at the cost of drilling into more chunks.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Selection
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME"
What you are about to do with the drive. A profile sets the mode and the
passes that go with it; any explicit flag overrides what it set, whichever
order the two appear in. The report names the profile that produced it.
.RS
.TP
.B predeploy
No data on it yet: write every sector and verify it reads back, in 1M chunks
with the write cache off for the run. It also leaves the drive configured for
service, which is the part that outlives the run:
.B \-\-fix\-config
and
.B \-\-bms on
with a weekly
.BR \-\-bms\-interval ,
all saved on a SAS drive. The default,
so a bare invocation stops and asks for
.B \-\-confirm
rather than doing anything.
.TP
.B inservice
It holds data you want to keep. Never writes anything.
.TP
.B survey
Quick triage of a shelf: samples the surface, read only.
.TP
.B decay
Weeks after a predeploy run: has the pattern rotted?
.TP
.B repair
predeploy, plus forcing a reallocation of anything unreadable.
.RE
.IP
The drive settings a profile carries apply only in its own mode.
.B \-\-mode read
under predeploy may be a drive in service, and saves nothing; nor do
.B \-\-apply\-settings
or
.BR \-\-format ,
which say exactly what they want done.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-tui
Interactive terminal UI. Implied when nothing to scan was named and both stdin
and stdout are a terminal. In the drive list,
.B space
toggles one drive,
.B a
selects every free HDD,
.B g
selects every free HDD on the same controller as the one under the cursor, and
.B n
clears the selection \(em picking a shelf of two hundred drives one at a time
is not a workflow. Passing it explicitly opens the form even with
devices named, which arrive pre-selected.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tui
Never open the UI, whatever the terminal looks like.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-deps
List the optional tools hddscan can use, whether each is installed, and the
.BR dnf (8)
and
.BR apt (8)
line that installs what is missing. Then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Show detected drives, their health-relevant facts, and whether each is safe to
test. Then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Scan every detected rotational, unmounted disk.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-ssd
Allow non-rotational devices. Latency thresholds will be meaningless.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-remote
Allow iSCSI, virtio, FibreChannel and Hyper-V disks. These report themselves as
rotational, but a clean result from one measures a network and someone else's
cache rather than a platter.
.SS Parallelism
Several drives are always scanned at once, one worker process per drive,
because separate spindles are genuinely independent. Work is never parallelised
inside a single drive: a second outstanding request makes the head seek away,
and the result would be a measurement of the queue rather than of the platter.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-parallel " N"
Cap concurrent drives. Useful when the HBA, expander or power supply cannot
feed every drive at full speed, which would otherwise show up as inflated
latency.
.TP
.B \-\-sequential
One drive at a time, full report straight to stdout.
.SS Runs that outlive the terminal
A scan or a low level format is a
.IR run .
It belongs to a supervisor process of its own rather than to the window that
started it, and every drive in it keeps a small text record of where it has
got to. Quit the program, close the terminal or lose the ssh session and the
work carries on; come back to it with
.B \-\-status
or
.BR \-\-attach .
Thirty drives being formatted and twenty-four being scanned are two runs, and
the dashboard swaps between them.
.PP
Records are written under
.I /var/lib/hddscan
when running as root and under
.I ~/.local/state/hddscan
otherwise. Finished runs are kept for a week, then pruned; a live one is never
pruned. On the dashboard,
.B q
leaves the run going,
.B r
returns to the list of runs,
.B n
starts another test and
.B x
stops the run after asking.
.SS Reading the dashboard
A drive is what it is doing \(em percentage, rate, bad and weak sector
counts, ETA and state \(em and then how it is behaving, which for a running
drive is its read latency over the last thirty seconds and for any other is
what the drive is. On a terminal wide enough for both, that is one row per
drive; otherwise it is two, with a rule between one drive and the next, and
the whole table fits eighty columns. The rate, count and ETA columns widen
to fit what is in them, so the header stays over its values.
.PP
More drives than the screen holds is the normal case on a shelf, so the table
pages:
.BR < " and " > ,
the left and right arrows, page up and page down, and home and end. The
footer says which page is showing.
.B g
swaps the table for an overview that puts every drive on one line as a name,
a percentage and a bar \(em less about each drive, but far more of them at
once \(em and swaps back. A run with more drives than the table can show
opens on the overview. The summary the run ends on pages with the same keys.
.TP
.B \-\-detach
Start the run and return to the shell, printing the run id. Nothing is watched
and nothing is waited for, so the exit status says only whether the run
started.
.TP
.BI \-\-status " [RUN|DRIVE]"
Print what every run on this machine is doing. With a run id, a prefix of one,
or the name of a drive in one, print the drives in that run instead. Needs no
privileges and nothing left running.
.TP
.BI \-\-status\-json " [RUN|DRIVE]"
The same thing as JSON, for a script or a monitoring check.
.TP
.BI \-\-attach " [RUN|DRIVE]"
Open the dashboard on a run that is already going. With no argument, show the
list of runs and pick one.
.TP
.BI \-\-stop " RUN" |all
Ask a run to stop. Every drive still writes the report for the part of it that
was covered, and the report says how much that was. A low level format cannot
be called back once the drive has begun it: stopping only stops watching, and
the record says so.
.TP
.BI \-\-forget " RUN" |finished
Drop the records of a finished run. Refuses a live one, and never touches the
reports themselves.
.TP
.BI \-\-state\-dir " DIR"
Keep run records here instead of in the default location. The environment
variable
.B HDDSCAN_STATE_DIR
does the same thing.
.SS Test mode
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " read"
Read-only surface scan; never writes. The mode of
.BR "\-\-profile inservice" ;
the default profile, predeploy, writes.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " verify"
Non-destructive write test: save the sector, write a pattern, read it back,
restore the original. Risks data loss on a power cut; see
.BR \-\-journal .
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " write"
Destructive. Overwrite everything with a pattern and verify it reads back
correctly.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " check"
Read-only re-verify of a pattern an earlier
.BI \-\-mode " write"
run left behind. Run it days or weeks later to catch sectors that lost their
charge.
.TP
.BI \-\-confirm " STRING"
Required for verify, write and format. Must equal the device name (for example
.IR sdb )
or its serial number.
.TP
.B \-\-repair
Give a drive its best chance at recovering, in one flag. Overwrites every
sector, which is what refreshes a weakly written one; rewrites anything still
slow after that; forces a reallocation on anything that will not read at all;
then re-reads the whole device to prove the repair held. Equivalent to
.BI \-\-mode " write"
with
.BR \-\-rewrite\-weak ,
.B \-\-force\-remap
and
.BR \-\-second\-pass ,
so it
.B destroys all data
and needs
.BR \-\-confirm .
It also turns the drive's read cache off, because a repair verified out of the
drive's own DRAM is not a repair you verified.
.TP
.B \-\-second\-pass
After
.BI \-\-mode " write" ,
re-read the whole device and re-verify the pattern, catching slow data decay.
.TP
.B \-\-rewrite\-weak
Rewrite readable-but-slow sectors in place so the drive refreshes or remaps
them. Needs verify or write mode, or read mode plus this flag for
read-modify-write. Has no effect unless the drive's automatic reallocation is
enabled; see
.BR \-\-awre .
.TP
.B \-\-force\-remap
Overwrite unreadable sectors with zeros to force a reallocation. Destroys the
contents of those sectors.
.SS Range and sizing
.TP
.BI \-\-start " SIZE"
First byte to test. Default 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-end " SIZE"
Last byte to test. Default is the end of the device.
.TP
.BI \-\-chunk " SIZE"
Bulk read size, default 128K, or 1M under predeploy and repair. The single biggest lever on how long a scan
takes, and on what it can see; see
.B Thresholds
above.
.TP
.BI \-\-block " SIZE"
Drill-down granularity, default 4K.
.TP
.BI \-\-sample " N"
Test only every Nth chunk, for a fast surface survey. The report states the
resulting coverage.
.TP
.BI \-\-order " ORDER"
.IR sequential " (default), " reverse " or " random .
Sequential reads at full streaming speed, but the drive is prefetching ahead,
so a marginal sector can be served from its buffer and look healthy. Random
defeats that entirely and is far slower; combine it with
.B \-\-sample
for a thorough spot check.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-time " SECONDS"
Stop after this long and report what was covered.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-errors " N"
Stop after N hard I/O errors. Default: never.
.SS Latency budget
.TP
.BI \-\-chunk\-slow\-ms " MS"
Chunk budget. Over this, hddscan drills into individual sectors.
.TP
.BI \-\-sector\-slow\-ms " MS"
Sector budget. Over this, the sector is retried. The chunk budget is clamped to
never exceed this, since a looser chunk budget would let slow sectors hide
inside chunks that are never examined.
.TP
.BI \-\-auto\-factor " F"
Automatic budget is F times the measured median. Default 3.
.TP
.BI \-\-floor\-ms " MS"
The automatic budget never goes below this. Default 25, plus an allowance for
the transfer time of a chunk larger than the default.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-rate " MIB"
A hard drive that tests slower than this many MiB/s at 4 MiB chunks is
FAILING, however clean its sectors, and one that spends a trailing ten-minute
window under it is SUSPECT. Smaller chunks get a proportionally lower
floor, verify mode three quarters of it. Judged over the whole scan once it has
run for two minutes; a drive under it shows
.B too slow
while scanning. Only rotational drives on a local bus are judged, and a USB
drive is SUSPECT rather than FAILING, since the link alone can be that slow.
0 turns it off. Default 10.
.TP
.BI \-\-retries " N"
Re-reads of a suspicious sector, default 20. A merely weak sector often reads
back fine after a few tries; that is the drive recovering, and it is worth
knowing about.
.TP
.BI \-\-retry\-max\-seconds " S"
Give up retrying one sector after this long. Default 60, 0 for no limit.
Without it, a drive whose every read hits the SCSI timeout would take minutes
per sector and never finish.
.SS Safety and environment
.TP
.B \-\-force
Test even if the drive looks mounted or otherwise in use.
.TP
.BI \-\-drive\-lookahead " off|keep"
.I off
(the default) tells the drive to stop prefetching into its own DRAM for the
duration of the scan, so a sequential pass cannot be handed a marginal sector
at cache speed. Restored on exit, on
.BR SIGINT ,
and on error. Needs
.BR hdparm (8)
on ATA/SATA and
.BR sdparm (8)
on SAS/SCSI; without the right one the scan says so and runs anyway.
.TP
.BI \-\-io\-timeout " SEC"
Lower the kernel's per-command timeout during the scan so a dying sector fails
fast. Restored afterwards.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-temp " C"
Abort if the drive gets this hot. Default 58, 0 to disable.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-smart
Do not call
.BR smartctl (8).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-direct
Do not use
.BR O_DIRECT .
Results will include page cache hits and cannot be trusted.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be done and touch nothing.
.SS Low level format
.TP
.B \-\-format
Issue a SCSI FORMAT UNIT to every selected drive, in parallel, with each
drive's progress tagged by name. This
.B erases the drive,
runs for hours, and cannot be undone; losing power partway through can leave a
drive needing vendor tooling to revive. Requires
.B \-\-confirm
and
.BR sg_format (8),
refuses any drive that is mounted or claimed, and prints the exact sg_format
command for every drive before anything starts.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints those commands and stops.
.TP
.BI \-\-format\-blocksize " 512|4096|keep"
Logical block size to format to. Default
.IR keep .
.TP
.B \-\-format\-fast
Ask for a fast format: the drive does not visit every block, which can turn
hours into minutes. Only meaningful when the physical sector layout is not
changing \(em a 512e drive already has 4096-byte physical sectors, so moving
the logical size to 4096 is a remapping rather than a re-lay-out. Blocks that
have never been written read without error afterwards. A drive may refuse it,
which costs nothing: the format simply does not start.
.TP
.BI \-\-format\-pi " none|keep|0..3"
Protection information to format with.
.I keep
preserves what the drive already reports and is the default, because sg_format
itself defaults to stripping it: a bare block size change would otherwise
remove protection information as a side effect nobody asked for.
.SS Drive settings
Settings are applied to every selected drive before anything else happens, not
when a scan worker eventually reaches its drive. Unless
.B \-\-persist
is given they are restored on exit, on
.BR SIGINT ,
and on error.
.TP
.B \-\-apply\-settings
Apply the settings below to every selected drive and stop, without scanning.
.TP
.B \-\-fix\-config
Apply the configuration hddscan recommends for a drive going into service:
automatic reallocation on for both reads and writes, read cache on. Only the
parts actually wrong are touched, each is printed, and they are saved to the
drive \(em a fix that reverted when the scan ended would not be a fix.
On by default under predeploy and repair. A field also named on its own with
.BR \-\-awre ,
.B \-\-arre
or
.B \-\-read\-cache
is left to that flag.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-fix\-config
Leave the configuration alone under the profiles that fix it by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-awre " on|off|keep"
Let the drive retire a sector it struggles to write.
.TP
.BI \-\-arre " on|off|keep"
The same on read. With both off the firmware never reallocates, so
.B \-\-rewrite\-weak
and
.B \-\-force\-remap
rewrite a bad sector straight back onto itself. Filesystem-level self-healing
does not substitute for this: ZFS repairs data from a redundant copy, but the
repair is written to the same LBA, and whether that sector is retired to a
spare is AWRE's decision alone.
.TP
.BI \-\-read\-cache " on|off|keep"
.I off
stops the drive serving any read from its own DRAM, which is stricter than
.BI \-\-drive\-lookahead " off" .
Left off on a drive in service it makes reads crawl; that is the classic
ex-array fault where a drive writes at full speed and reads like treacle.
.TP
.BI \-\-write\-cache " on|off|keep"
With the cache on, a write returns as soon as the drive has the data in DRAM,
so a write mode times the cache rather than the platter.
.I off
makes it honest and much slower. Restored on exit, and saved by
.B \-\-persist
only when named here. Default
.I off
under predeploy and repair,
.I keep
otherwise.
.TP
.BI \-\-drive\-read\-retries " N"
Bound how many times the drive re-reads a sector internally before reporting
it (the SCSI read retry count, 0 to 255). This is the count-based twin of
.BR \-\-recovery\-time ,
which bounds the same thing by time; firmware differs in which of the two it
actually honours, so setting both is the reliable way to make a drive fail
fast. Not to be confused with
.BR \-\-retries ,
which is how many times
.I this tool
re-reads a sector.
.TP
.BI \-\-drive\-write\-retries " N"
The same for writes.
.TP
.BI \-\-recovery\-time " MS"
Bound how long the drive fights one bad sector before reporting it (the SCSI
recovery time limit; the equivalent of ATA's SCT ERC or TLER). 0 means
unlimited, which is what makes a dying disk stall for minutes and a ZFS or RAID
member get faulted for being slow rather than broken. SAS only.
.TP
.BI \-\-bms " on|keep"
.I on
tells every selected SAS/SCSI drive to run its own background medium scan: the
firmware sweeps the platters whenever the drive is idle, logs what it finds,
and reallocates the sectors it can still recover. Unlike every other setting
here this one is
.B saved on the drive
and is not undone afterwards;
.B sdparm \-\-clear=EN_BMS \-\-save /dev/sdX
turns it back off. Default
.I on
under predeploy and repair,
.I keep
otherwise.
.TP
.BI \-\-bms\-interval " HOURS"
How often that background scan sweeps the media (the SCSI BMS_I field), saved
with it. Default 168, weekly, under predeploy and repair; otherwise the drive's
own. Needs
.BR "\-\-bms on" .
.TP
.B \-\-persist
Save the settings this run changes to the drive so they survive a power cycle,
instead of putting them back when the scan ends. Needs a SAS/SCSI drive and
.BR sdparm (8);
anything else is applied for the run only and says so. Only settings named
explicitly are saved \(em the look-ahead, read cache and write cache defaults are
not, or every scanned drive would be left permanently slower.
.SS Output
.TP
.BI \-\-json " FILE"
Write a machine readable report.
.TP
.BI \-\-badblocks\-list " FILE"
Write bad sectors in
.BR badblocks (8)
format, ready for
.B mke2fs \-l
so a new filesystem steps around them. Includes every sector the scan would
not trust: bad, corrupt and weak alike.
.TP
.BI \-\-badblocks\-from " FILE"
Build that list from a scan that has already finished, reading its checkpoint
.RB ( \-\-state ,
or the one a parallel run keeps for each drive) or its
.BR \-\-csv .
No drive is opened, so the block size and the partition offset can be chosen
after the scan, once the partition they are for exists. Requires
.BR \-\-badblocks\-list .
.TP
.B \-\-hide\-bad
Write a map of the damage found by the newest finished scan of the whole drive
onto the drive, and activate
.IR /dev/mapper/bb\-<serial> :
the same drive with the damage cut out, which any filesystem can use. A
sampled or interrupted scan is not used; a list from one would hide only the
damage that happened to be looked at. Whatever is on the drive becomes
unreachable, so it needs
.B \-\-confirm
exactly as a write mode does. The drive is found in the run store by serial,
since its name can change between boots.
.TP
.BI \-\-badblocks\-blocksize " N"
Unit for that list. Default 1024. Use the block size the filesystem will be
made with, usually 4096.
.TP
.BI \-\-badblocks\-offset " N"
Subtract a partition's start offset from the block numbers, since
.BR mke2fs (8)
runs on the partition rather than the whole disk.
.TP
.BI \-\-prometheus " FILE"
Write metrics for node_exporter's textfile collector.
.TP
.BI \-\-journal " FILE"
Crash journal for
.BI \-\-mode " verify" .
The original bytes are fsynced here before the pattern is written, so a power
cut mid-chunk can be undone by the next run.
.TP
.BI \-\-csv " FILE"
Write every suspect sector as CSV.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " FILE"
Tee the report to a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-state " FILE"
Checkpoint progress here every 15 seconds. With several drives the device name
is appended, so each gets a file of its own; one file shared by a whole
parallel run would have every worker overwriting the others. Without this
option each drive still checkpoints inside its run's directory, which is where
.B \-\-status
says the records are.
.TP
.B \-\-resume
Continue from the checkpoint in
.BR \-\-state .
Worth having on any multi-day run.
.TP
.BI \-\-outdir " DIR"
Where per-device reports go when several drives are scanned at once.
.TP
.BI \-\-map\-width " N"
Width of the surface map. Default 64.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
No progress output.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Plain text.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option summary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH DEVICE-MAPPER MAPS
.B hddscan dm
keeps a map of a drive's bad extents on the drive itself and loads it as a
.B dm\-linear
table, so a filesystem sees a slightly smaller device with none of the known
damage in it. No kernel module is involved. Installed as a link named
.BR dm\-badblocks ,
the same binary answers to that name.
.PP
The drive is divided into extents. The first and last hold two copies of the
map, each with a generation number and a checksum; an update writes one, syncs
it, then writes the other, and a read takes the newest that checks out. Every
Kth extent is a spare. Damage known when the map is made is skipped, keeping
the device in platter order; damage found later is remapped to the nearest
spare, since skipping it then would move every byte after it.
.TP
.BI create " DEV " \-\-bad " FILE " \-\-confirm " DEV"
Write a new map. Refuses a drive that already has one
.RB ( \-\-force
replaces it, losing everything on the mapped device) and a drive with damage
where the map's two copies would live.
.TP
.BI activate " DEV" "\fR, \fPactivate\-all"
Load the map as
.IR /dev/mapper/NAME .
.B activate\-all
finds every drive carrying a map, by the map rather than by name, and is what
.I contrib/dm\-badblocks.service
runs at boot, before local filesystems and ZFS imports.
.TP
.BI remap " DEV " \-\-bad " FILE"
Move damage found later to spares. On a live device it suspends it, copies
what can still be read, checks the copy past the page cache, writes the map,
reloads the table and resumes; if the reload fails the previous map is written
back so the drive never disagrees with the kernel. Sectors that could not be
read are zeroed and their range on the mapped device is printed.
.TP
.BI status " DEV" "\fR, \fPtable" " DEV" "\fR, \fPmap" " DEV OFFSET" "\fR, \fPdeactivate" " NAME"
Show the map, print the dm table, translate an offset, remove the device.
.TP
.BI \-\-bad " FILE"
Bad blocks in
.BR badblocks (8)
format, relative to the whole device;
.B \-
is standard input.
.TP
.BI \-\-block\-size " N"
Unit of the numbers in that file. Default 1024; 512 for sector numbers from the
kernel log.
.TP
.BI \-\-extent " SIZE"
Granularity of the map, a power of two from 64K to 64M. Default 1M.
.TP
.BI \-\-reserve " PCT"
Share of the drive held as spares. Default 0.1, one extent in a thousand.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
Device-mapper name. Default
.BI bb\- device\fR;
.B \-\-hide\-bad
uses the drive's serial.
.TP
.B \-\-logical
For
.BR remap :
the file numbers blocks of the mapped device rather than of the drive.
.TP
.B \-\-offline
For
.BR remap :
the device is not active. Refused if it is.
.TP
.B \-\-physical
For
.BR map :
the offset is on the drive, not on the mapped device.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Healthy. Every sector returned data within the latency budget.
.TP
.B 1
Suspect. Sectors were unreadable or needed retries; uncorrectable errors,
reallocations, command timeouts or SAS delayed
corrections grew during the scan, the drive has needed spin-up retries, the
kernel logged a timeout or reset against the drive, a band of
the surface read at more than twice what calibration expected there, the drive
spent ten minutes under
.BR \-\-min\-rate ,
or more
than one chunk read or write in a thousand went over the latency budget.
.TP
.B 2
Failing, or a usage error. A drive that tested under
.BR \-\-min\-rate ,
that disappeared mid-scan, that the kernel took offline or reset three or more
times during the scan, whose end-to-end error counter grew during it, that
returned the wrong data, or that could not read a sector even after writing it,
is failing too. Unreadable sectors on their own are not: they make a drive
suspect until a write shows whether it can still remap them.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.
.PP
The exit status reflects the drive, not the coverage. A HEALTHY verdict over
3% of a drive is not a healthy drive; read the
.B Coverage
line of the report.
.SH PROTECTION INFORMATION
SAS drives are often formatted with T10 protection information: eight extra
bytes beside every logical block holding a CRC guard and tags. The kernel then
reads with RDPROTECT set, so the drive checks that guard on every read.
.PP
A block that has never been written since the format has no valid guard. Reading
it fails immediately with
.BR EILSEQ ,
even though the platter under it is perfect. On a freshly formatted drive that
is most of the device, and a read-only scan will stop at the exact offset the
last write reached.
.PP
hddscan reads the protection type from sysfs, shows it in
.B \-\-list
and in the report, counts such refusals separately from bad sectors, and states
in the verdict that those blocks were never tested. A destructive
.BI \-\-mode " write"
pass repairs them: writing a block is what generates its guard tag, so the same
run that tests the surface also makes it readable.
.BI \-\-mode " verify"
cannot do this, because it must read the original bytes before it may write.
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan every free rotational disk, read-only:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hddscan \-\-all
.EE
.RE
.PP
A quick surface survey of one drive, defeating the drive's prefetch:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hddscan \-\-sample 64 \-\-order random /dev/sdb
.EE
.RE
.PP
Full destructive surface test with a checkpoint, on a drive that is not
carrying data:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hddscan \-\-mode write \-\-confirm sdb \-\-chunk 4M \e
        \-\-state /var/tmp/sdb.state \-\-resume /dev/sdb
.EE
.RE
.PP
Prepare a shelf of ex-array drives without scanning them:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hddscan \-\-apply\-settings \-\-fix\-config \-\-recovery\-time 7000 \e
        \-\-bms on /dev/sdb /dev/sdc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Feed the bad sectors of a partition to a new filesystem:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hddscan \-\-badblocks\-list bb.txt \-\-badblocks\-offset 1M /dev/sdb
mke2fs \-l bb.txt /dev/sdb1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Put a drive with a few bad sectors back to work, from a scan that has
already finished. The end of scan report prints these commands with the
numbers filled in:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hddscan \-\-badblocks\-from /var/lib/hddscan/RUN/sdb.ckpt \e
        \-\-badblocks\-list /root/sdb.bb \e
        \-\-badblocks\-blocksize 4096 \-\-badblocks\-offset 1M
mkfs.ext4 \-b 4096 \-l /root/sdb.bb /dev/sdb1
e2fsck \-l /root/sdb.bb /dev/sdb1      # add more, later
.EE
.RE
.PP
Only ext2/3/4 can do this: the listed blocks go in the bad block inode and
nothing is ever allocated inside it. XFS and btrfs have no equivalent, and
.BR mdadm (8)
and ZFS fault a drive out on a read error rather than stepping around it, so
a drive kept this way belongs somewhere a second copy exists \(em a backup
target or a scratch disk \(em and not in an array or a pool.
.PP
For any other filesystem,
.B \-\-hide\-bad
cuts the damage out below it instead, with device-mapper, and the filesystem
goes on
.IR /dev/mapper/bb\-<serial> .
That is also what makes ZFS reasonable on such a drive: the pool never sees the
damage the scan found, and its checksums catch whatever appears later.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR ./hddscan- <device>.txt
Per-device report when several drives are scanned at once. See
.BR \-\-outdir .
.TP
.IR ./hddscan-format- <device>.txt
Everything sg_format said while formatting that drive.
.TP
.I /var/lib/hddscan/runs/<run>/
One directory per run, holding the run's own record, one
.I <device>.job
record per drive, that drive's checkpoint, and
.I log
for anything the run had to say on the way past. Under
.I ~/.local/state/hddscan
when not running as root, or wherever
.B \-\-state\-dir
says. The records are plain key-and-value text, rewritten through a temporary
file and renamed, so reading one with grep while a scan is running is safe.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
hddscan links nothing beyond libc, so the binary works on a rescue system with
no packages installed. The following are shelled out to when present and done
without when absent;
.B \-\-check\-deps
reports which are missing and how to install them.
.TP
.BR smartctl (8)
SMART health, temperature, and the drive's own error counters.
.TP
.BR sdparm (8)
Every drive setting on SAS/SCSI: look-ahead, caches, reallocation, recovery
time, background scan.
.TP
sg3_utils
SCSI mode pages, sector reassignment, and reformatting.
.TP
.BR hdparm (8)
Look-ahead and write cache on ATA/SATA drives.
.SH CAVEATS
Latency is only meaningful on an otherwise idle drive. Any other I/O to the
same spindle shows up as slow sectors. The
.B O_EXCL
open prevents mounting but not a competing raw reader.
.PP
Several code paths have not been exercised on real hardware and are documented
as such in DESIGN.md rather than glossed over. Do not read a clean result from
an untested path as evidence that the path works.
